New York could become the first major US  city to cap the number of cars used by  ride-sharing companies after years of  complaints from taxi owners protesters  rallied yesterday ahead of today's  scheduled City Council vote on limiting  companies like uber and lyft Kris Van  Cleave is at Penn Station one of the  commuter hubs here in Manhattan Chris  good morning to you good morning as uber  and lyft have gotten more popular and  really soared in popularity cab drivers  have been lamenting how much harder it  is to make a living back there have been  at least six driver suicides in recent  months some linked to financial woes  it's really driving this legislative  effort to cap uber and lyft now there  are more than 13,500 licensed taxis in  the city but more than a hundred  thousand licensed drivers for ride  hailing apps the proposed legislation  would cap the number of those ride  hailing app drivers at its current level  while the city studies their impact also  being considered is imposing a minimum  wage for drivers that would also be a  first in the nation the mayor and many  drivers support the proposals but uber  and lyft say limiting their drivers will  hurt minority communities that do not  have reliable mass transit they also  claim it would mean longer wait times  and reduce service now all of this comes  as a recent study found ride-sharing  apps are making traffic worse by  encouraging people not to take mass  transit here is a sobering stat about  the sign of the time see this medallion  here on the hood of the cab you need  that to be a New York City cab driver  those used to go for up to a million  dollars apiece now they're worth around  $200,000 Vlad Kris Van Cleave thank you  very much it's a difficult story because  there's arguments to be made on both  sides do indeed
 

